1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,491
Welcome to Class 13: RCC storage architecture.

2
00:00:03,491 --> 00:00:07,980
This video introduces the core decisions and working patterns.

3
00:00:07,980 --> 00:00:14,963
Watch the complete lesson first, then use the written class page for copyable commands,

4
00:00:14,963 --> 00:00:17,456
exercises, and detailed reference material.

5
00:00:17,456 --> 00:00:23,836
After this class, you should be able to: describe the roles of Redis,

6
00:00:23,836 --> 00:00:31,688
MinIO, and the JuiceFS client; distinguish metadata traffic from file-content traffic; explain why small-file workloads differ

7
00:00:31,688 --> 00:00:39,049
from large streaming workloads; understand the RCC 100 Gb/s server and 10 Gb/s client topology;

8
00:00:39,049 --> 00:00:45,428
explain client and container caching; and choose appropriate I/O patterns for scientific workflows.

9
00:00:45,428 --> 00:00:51,439
Redis stores information such as: paths and directory entries; ownership and permissions;

10
00:00:51,439 --> 00:00:58,952
file sizes and timestamps; inode-like identifiers; object mappings; locks and coordination state; and filesystem bookkeeping.

11
00:00:58,952 --> 00:01:06,966
Operations such as stat, ls, open, rename, mkdir, and unlink may transfer almost no file content

12
00:01:06,966 --> 00:01:09,470
while still generating metadata requests.

13
00:01:09,470 --> 00:01:17,484
This makes workloads with hundreds of thousands of files fundamentally different from workloads containing a few

14
00:01:17,484 --> 00:01:18,486
large files.

15
00:01:18,486 --> 00:01:22,994
Increasing S3 bandwidth does not remove a metadata bottleneck.

16
00:01:22,994 --> 00:01:28,637
MinIO provides the S3-compatible object-storage layer used for durable file contents.

17
00:01:28,637 --> 00:01:36,846
Object storage is effective for: large objects; sequential reads and writes; durable replicated or erasure-coded storage;

18
00:01:36,846 --> 00:01:40,951
checksummed content; and aggregate access from many clients.

19
00:01:40,951 --> 00:01:46,594
The JuiceFS client translates filesystem reads and writes into object operations.

20
00:01:46,594 --> 00:01:49,673
Large streaming transfers amortize request overhead.

21
00:01:49,673 --> 00:01:56,855
Tiny, scattered reads and writes can generate many requests for relatively little useful data.

22
00:01:56,855 --> 00:02:01,929
Storage and backend servers have connectivity of up to 100 Gb/s.

23
00:02:01,929 --> 00:02:05,157
Typical compute clients have 10 Gb/s links.

24
00:02:05,157 --> 00:02:08,847
The 100 Gb/s server side is aggregate capacity.

25
00:02:08,847 --> 00:02:13,459
It does not provide 100 Gb/s to one compute node.

26
00:02:13,459 --> 00:02:20,838
Ten clients each attempting to sustain 10 Gb/s can already approach the nominal capacity of one

27
00:02:20,838 --> 00:02:22,683
100 Gb/s backend link.

28
00:02:22,683 --> 00:02:28,679
Actual useful throughput is lower because of: protocol overhead; request latency; metadata operations;

29
00:02:28,679 --> 00:02:32,830
contention; checksums and encryption; filesystem translation; and application processing.

30
00:02:32,830 --> 00:02:39,748
Random and small-file workloads often do not fill a 10 Gb/s link with useful data.

31
00:02:39,748 --> 00:02:44,821
They spend their time waiting for network round trips and metadata.

32
00:02:44,821 --> 00:02:52,415
Large compressed FASTQ, BAM, CRAM, archives, and model files generally support efficient streaming and readahead.

33
00:02:52,415 --> 00:02:58,490
Many small files require repeated: path lookups; permission checks; opens and closes;

34
00:02:58,490 --> 00:03:03,046
metadata updates; object requests; directory updates; and cache bookkeeping.

35
00:03:03,046 --> 00:03:11,146
Twenty gigabytes stored in 500,000 files can therefore perform much worse than one 20 GB archive.

36
00:03:11,146 --> 00:03:18,234
Conda environments are a common example: startup may require opening thousands of small files.

37
00:03:18,234 --> 00:03:26,334
This is one reason RCC prefers Apptainer images over Conda environments stored on shared network filesystems.

38
00:03:26,334 --> 00:03:32,654
The JuiceFS client may cache active data in memory and on node-local disk.

39
00:03:32,654 --> 00:03:39,460
A cache hit avoids another object download: A cache miss uses the complete path:

40
00:03:39,460 --> 00:03:45,780
Caching helps when stable data is reused, for example: Apptainer images; reference genomes;

41
00:03:45,780 --> 00:03:50,642
aligner indexes; annotation databases; model weights; and read-only database snapshots.

42
00:03:50,642 --> 00:03:55,989
Cache entries should be identified by immutable version, digest, or checksum.

43
00:03:55,989 --> 00:04:00,365
Mutable names such as latest.sif are unsafe cache identities.

44
00:04:00,365 --> 00:04:07,753
A parent job that stages data or warms a cache on one node should not submit

45
00:04:07,753 --> 00:04:14,218
child jobs that may run elsewhere: Nested submission destroys locality and can create repeated:

46
00:04:14,218 --> 00:04:20,220
S3 downloads; Redis metadata traffic; 10 Gb/s client-link use; container transfers; reference-index transfers;

47
00:04:20,220 --> 00:04:22,067
and synchronized backend load.

48
00:04:22,067 --> 00:04:28,994
Use job arrays, explicit Slurm dependencies, or Snakemake's Slurm executor from an approved submission host.

49
00:04:28,994 --> 00:04:33,612
Each compute job should receive a complete unit of work.

50
00:04:33,612 --> 00:04:40,696
When reporting a problem, provide the Slurm job ID, node, path, approximate file count,

51
00:04:40,696 --> 00:04:46,262
total size, read/write pattern, and whether local staging changes the result.

52
00:04:46,262 --> 00:04:48,792
Do not include patient identifiers.

53
00:04:48,792 --> 00:04:55,338
Given one streaming workload and one small-file or random-I/O workload, trace the likely metadata,

54
00:04:55,338 --> 00:04:57,676
object-storage, network, and cache paths.

55
00:04:57,676 --> 00:05:03,286
State which data should remain durable, which work should use job-local scratch,

56
00:05:03,286 --> 00:05:07,027
and what you would measure before changing resources.

